      "author": "Manuel São Bento",
      "content": "Check out my full review @ https://movieswetextedabout.com/the-last-house-movie-review-louis-leterrier-builds-a-remarkable-house-but-story-misses-its-furnishing/\r\n\r\nRating: C+\r\n\r\nTHE LAST HOUSE is a film of two very different halves — a sluggish, oddly toneless first hour that squanders a genuinely intriguing premise, followed by a back stretch that finally locates the tension and craft the setup always promised, only to rush its way to an ending more interested in sequel-baiting than resolution. Greta Lee and Wagner Moura are doing real work here, and the fully built, physically degrading house is a legitimate technical achievement that elevates every scene shot inside it, but the script never digs past the surface of its own ideas, leaving genuinely rich thematic territory — isolation, technological dependency, what security actually costs a family — almost entirely unexplored. It’s a divisive movie, one that’s likely to frustrate as many people as it entertains, and it’s easy to see why Netflix sat on the embargo until the last possible moment.",
      "rating": 5,
      "createdAt": "2026-08-07T07:04:58Z"
    },
    {
      "author": "Sierbahnn",
      "content": "This movie changes from a psychological drama about isolation to a full-on survivor horror movie and to then pivot into a near cosmic horror story at the very end. It is an interesting transition and one that not many movies could pull off. This one does it because you are invested in the characters and they are so well acted and so well directed that the premise actualizes them rather than diminish them. The adversarial conditions that the characters are placed in are positioned in the story very well and are given enough good effects so that they become quite terrifying. It is not a movie for the ages possibly, but it is certainly good enough to give it a watch.",
      "rating": 7,
      "createdAt": "2026-08-09T09:02:49Z"
    },
    {
      "author": "ugrobug",
      "content": "At first glance, Leterrier seems to have made a belated pandemic thriller, where the home becomes a prison and isolation feels more terrifying than any alien invader. But dig a little deeper, and the film reveals itself as a story about an inner void that gradually crystallizes amid the constant noise of the information age. News comes and goes, morning headlines are replaced by fresh sensations before nightfall, and we barely notice as the world quietly turns upside down. What begins as a forced digital detox unexpectedly becomes an invitation to rediscover life’s simplest pleasures: playing a board game, watching a movie together as a family (although the selection is rather limited—there are only two films to choose from), or simply ending the evening with a good old-fashioned pillow fight.\r\n\r\n[Full version](https://tambur.pub/en/b/post/21990-posledniy-dom-s-vagnerom-mouroy-pod-kryshey-doma-svoego)",
      "rating": null,
      "createdAt": "2026-08-17T08:49:39Z"
    }
